Questions:
{
  "questions": [
    {
      "source_id": "1",
      "stem": "A search-and-rescue drone reports its position relative to the command post located at the origin. At a certain moment its position vector is $\\vec{r}= -7\\hat{i}+9\\hat{j}$, where distances are measured in kilometers. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The drone is $16\\ \\text{km}$ from the command post.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This corresponds to adding component magnitudes ($7+9$) instead of using the Euclidean distance formula."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The drone is approximately $11.4\\ \\text{km}$ from the command post.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The distance from the origin is the magnitude of the position vector: $|\\vec{r}|=\\sqrt{(-7)^2+9^2}=\\sqrt{130}\\approx 11.4\\ \\text{km}$. This uses the Pythagorean theorem for perpendicular components in the $x$- and $y$-directions."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The drone is $9\\ \\text{km}$ from the command post.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly treats the $y$-component ($9$ km) as the full distance from the origin."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The drone is $7\\ \\text{km}$ from the command post.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly treats the $x$-component magnitude ($7$ km) as the full distance from the origin."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "2",
      "stem": "A cart moves along the $x$-axis with displacement given by $\\vec{s}(t)=\\Big(\\left(0.80\\,\\mathrm{m/s^3}\\right)t^3+\\left(4.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}\\right)t^2\\Big)\\hat{i}$. Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0\\ \\text{s}$ is $9.0\\ \\text{m/s}^2$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Differentiate twice: $\\vec{v}(t)=\\dfrac{d\\vec{s}}{dt}=(2.40t^2+9.0t)\\hat{i}$ and $\\vec{a}(t)=\\dfrac{d\\vec{v}}{dt}=(4.8t+9.0)\\hat{i}$. Evaluating at $t=0$ gives $|\\vec{a}(0)|=9.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "This motion has constant acceleration.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Taking the second derivative gives $a(t)=4.8t+9.0$, which depends on $t$, so the acceleration is not constant."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration is $4.5\\ \\text{m/s}^2$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "The coefficient $4.5\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$ appears in the displacement function, but acceleration comes from the second derivative, not from reading off this coefficient."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0\\ \\text{s}$ is $0\\ \\text{m/s}^2$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "A common error is assuming an object starts with zero acceleration; here $a(0)=2\\times 4.5=9.0\\ \\mathrm{m/s^2}$ from $\\vec{a}(t)=\\dfrac{d^2\\vec{s}}{dt^2}$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "3",
      "stem": "A particle moves in the $xy$-plane from point A at $\\vec{r}_A=-3\\hat{i}+4\\hat{j}$ to point B at $\\vec{r}_B=5\\hat{i}-8\\hat{j}$ in $4.0\\ \\text{s}$ (coordinates in meters). Which statement is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The straight-line distance between A and B is $20\\ \\text{m}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This comes from adding component changes ($|\\Delta x|+|\\Delta y|=8+12$) instead of using $\\sqrt{(\\Delta x)^2+(\\Delta y)^2}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The speed at $t=0$ must be $3.6\\ \\text{m/s}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Computing $|\\Delta \\vec{r}|/\\Delta t$ gives the magnitude of average velocity, not the instantaneous speed at $t=0$; many motions can share the same endpoints and time."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The straight-line distance between A and B is approximately $14.4\\ \\text{m}$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Compute displacement: $\\Delta\\vec{r}=\\vec{r}_B-\\vec{r}_A=(5-(-3))\\hat{i}+(-8-4)\\hat{j}=8\\hat{i}-12\\hat{j}$. The straight-line separation is $|\\Delta\\vec{r}|=\\sqrt{8^2+(-12)^2}=\\sqrt{208}\\approx 14.4\\ \\text{m}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The acceleration at $t=0$ must be $0.90\\ \\text{m/s}^2$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Acceleration at a particular time cannot be uniquely determined from only initial/final positions and elapsed time without extra information (e.g., full trajectory or a constant-acceleration constraint plus more data)."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

Create a complete LaTeX document with:
1. A document preamble with necessary packages
2. Custom macros for question formatting (\QBlock, \Correct, \Wrong)
3. All questions formatted using the macros
4. Proper math formatting for all expressions

Set filename to "isomorphic_questions.tex"

